  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 115 PM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A dry afternoon is expected today as low pressure shifts \neastward into the Canadian Maritimes, and high pressure over the \nMississippi River Valley builds into the northeastern U.S. \nSeasonable temperatures currently in the mid 40s to lower 50s are \nexpected to fall as we head into the overnight hours, dropping to \nthe mid 30s to lower 40s by early Monday morning, which is \nrelatively mild for this time of year. West-northwesterly winds will \ndecrease into the overnight as well, though westerly gusts may \ncontinue at higher elevations with a modest low level jet overhead. \nPrecipitation chances also increase again this evening and tonight \nas we remain under the influence of a large upper level low rotating \nover eastern Canada. The first shortwave it sends our way will move \noverhead tonight and Monday. Much like our recent round of \nprecipitation, most of the shower activity will fall as rain, with \nsome light snow possible across the higher terrain and portions of \nthe Adirondacks/Northeast Kingdom, especially tonight. Any snow \naccumulation will be minimal below 2500 ft. Rain will be the name of \nthe game on Monday with highs in the 40s and lower 50s again. \nPrecipitation amounts are expected to be up to 0.20\" at most, \nhighest in the northern Greens. Winds could also be slightly breezy \nagain Monday out of the northwest, but not overly impactful. After \nbrief ridging Monday night and lows in the mid 20s to mid 30s, the \nnext shortwave should strike on Tuesday, though precipitation \namounts are expected to be lighter, up to 0.15\" at most. Once again, \nTuesday's seasonable highs in the upper 40s to mid 50s will limit \nmuch snow during the day, then overnight lows in the upper 20s and \n30s could allow for some light snow at higher elevations and cold \nspots. Tuesday's winds will be out of the southwest and slightly \nbreezy, but again not overly impactful. On Wednesday, we'll be \ndrying out with highs in the 40s to mid 50s and winds out of the \nwest-northwest again.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A period of unsettled weather looks to continue late \nnext week into the weekend, with additional chances for \nprecipitation expected. A lage-scale trough is expected to develop \nacross the eastern CONUS, with a developing surface low centered \nover the Ohio Valley, with a strengthening secondary low expected to \ndevelop and move through the Mid-Atlantic towards the New England \nCoast. Temperatures look to trend colder towards the weekend, with \nhighs only in the 30s and low 40s. Model guidance continues to \nstruggle with the expected low track, which will greatly influence \nhow much precipitation northern New York and Vermont can expect, as \nwell as precipitation type. Guidance is still rather split on whether \nthe low will be suppressed far enough south that we miss out or if it \nwill bring widespread precipitation to the region, but trends \ngenerally support a wetter solution although there is still even \nmore uncertainty regarding the expected precipitation type. The \nlatest guidance supports the higher terrain seeing their first \nsignificant snowfall of the season, while valley locations may \nremain as all rain this weekend. With this system still several days \nout, model outcomes are likely to change, with details becoming \nclearer over the next few days.",
